<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Right Triangle Using Its Two Legs</h2>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />. Apply <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{48}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The result names the total flat surface enclosed by the three sides: twenty-four square units. This is exactly what the Right Triangle Area From Legs Calculator returns for these two inputs.</p>
  <p>The calculator needs only the two legs, the sides that meet at the right angle. Because those two sides are perpendicular, one serves as the base and the other as the height, so no further measurement is required. The hypotenuse plays no part here. Multiply the legs, halve the product, and the area follows from <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>This is one of the simplest area calculations in geometry, because both perpendicular measurements are already in hand. It appears wherever a corner is square: construction layouts, cut sheets, garden beds, and survey sketches all reduce to two legs and one halved product.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Right triangle anatomy -->
<section id="sec-anatomy" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Before the formula makes sense, the labels must be exact. Every symbol below appears in the calculator, and each one carries a single, fixed meaning.</p>

  <h3>Definition of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a three-sided polygon containing exactly one interior angle of 90 degrees. It has three vertices, three sides, and three interior angles that sum to 180 degrees. The right angle is usually marked with a small square at the corner. The two sides forming that corner are the legs; the side opposite is the hypotenuse. Since <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />: the two acute angles are complementary, so knowing one determines the other.</p>

  <h3>What Are the Two Legs of a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>The legs are the two sides that meet at the right angle. Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. Their defining property is perpendicularity: <span set:html={rich('[[a\\perp b]]')} />. That single fact is what makes the area calculation so direct, because a perpendicular pair already supplies a base and a height with no extra construction.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the 90-degree angle. It is always the longest side of the triangle, and it never forms the right angle itself; it stretches across from it. It is not required in the legs-based area formula. It becomes useful when a side is missing, since <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} /> recovers it through the Pythagorean theorem, but the area calculator never asks for it.</p>

  <h3>Why Are the Two Legs Perpendicular?</h3>
  <p>Perpendicular means meeting at exactly 90 degrees. The legs share the right-angle vertex, so the corner between them is square by definition. This matters because area needs a base multiplied by a perpendicular height. In a right triangle that pair is already drawn: no altitude to drop, no extra line to construct. The triangle hands over both measurements directly.</p>

  <h3>Can the Legs Be Swapped?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Because <span set:html={rich('[[a\\times b=b\\times a]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{a\\times b}{2}=\\frac{b\\times a}{2}]]')} />. A concrete check: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{4\\times3}{2}=6]]')} />. Relabeling which leg is called <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and which is called <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> changes nothing about the triangle or its area. Enter the two measurements in either input; the calculator returns the same result.</p>

