<!-- Section 3: Base and height -->
<section id="sec-base-height" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Are the Two Legs the Base and Height?</h2>
  <p>The whole method rests on one geometric fact: in a right triangle, the base and the perpendicular height are already drawn as the two legs. Understanding why turns the formula from a memorized rule into something obvious.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Base of a Triangle?</h3>
  <p>The base is the side selected as the reference for the area calculation. Any side of a triangle can serve as the base. For a right triangle, choosing either leg as the base is natural, because the other leg is already perpendicular to it. The choice is a convenience: pick one leg as the base and the geometry of the right angle does the rest.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Perpendicular Height?</h3>
  <p>The height is the shortest distance from the base to the opposite vertex, measured at right angles to the base. When the base is leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the opposite vertex is the far end of leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> itself is that perpendicular distance. Nothing needs to be drawn or measured beyond the triangle; the height is a side, not a construction.</p>

  <h3>Why Does Perpendicularity Matter?</h3>
  <p>Multiplying two side lengths produces a base-times-height product only when the sides are perpendicular. A sloping side paired with a base measures a longer diagonal stretch, not the perpendicular height, so the product overshoots the true base-height rectangle. The legs of a right triangle are perpendicular by definition, which is exactly why their product is the correct starting point. This calculator requires the two sides forming the right angle; any other pairing breaks the logic the formula depends on.</p>

  <h3>Can the Hypotenuse Be Used as the Base?</h3>
  <p>A triangle can use any side as its base, including the hypotenuse, but the height must then be the perpendicular distance from the opposite vertex to that side. That distance is not one of the legs, and this calculator does not measure it. The hypotenuse cannot simply replace a leg in <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />; doing so computes a different, incorrect quantity. This calculator is built for the two perpendicular legs only.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Deriving the main area formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is the Area of a Right Triangle Half the Product of Its Legs?</h2>
  <p>Start with the general rule: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}}{2}]]')} />. For a right triangle, set <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Base}=a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Height}=b]]')} />. Because the two legs are perpendicular, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{a\\times b}{2}}]]]')} />. The rest of this section shows where that halving comes from, so the division by two is understood, not just remembered.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Rectangle Method</h3>
  <p>Draw a rectangle with width <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Its area is <span set:html={rich('[[K_{\\text{rectangle}}=a\\times b]]')} />. Now draw a diagonal from one corner to the opposite corner. The diagonal cuts the rectangle into two triangles that are exact copies of each other, each with legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and a right angle where the rectangle&apos;s corner was. Both triangles have equal area, so each owns half the rectangle: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\text{triangle}}=\\frac{a\\times b}{2}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Why Do Two Identical Right Triangles Form a Rectangle?</h3>
  <p>Place two copies of the triangle so their hypotenuses meet. The matching legs line up: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> against <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> against <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Because the acute angles of a right triangle are complementary (<span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />), the angles that join at each corner of the combined shape add up to 90 degrees all around. The result has four right angles and opposite sides equal: a rectangle. Its sides are exactly the two legs, which is why the rectangle&apos;s area is <span set:html={rich('[[a\\times b]]')} /> and each triangle owns half.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Division by Two Necessary?</h3>
  <p>Take the 6-by-8 rectangle. Its area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=6\\times8=48]]')} />. Each of the two identical right triangles occupies half: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{48}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24}]]]')} />. Skipping the division answers a different question: the rectangle&apos;s area, not the triangle&apos;s. The division by two is not a fudge factor; it is the step that trims the enclosing rectangle down to the triangle.</p>

  <h3>Geometric Proof Using Coordinates</h3>
  <p>For readers comfortable with coordinates, here is the same result from algebra. Place the right-angle vertex at the origin and let the vertices be <span set:html={rich('[[C=(0,0)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A=(a,0)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B=(0,b)]]')} />. The triangle is the region bounded by the two coordinate axes and the line joining <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. The base along the x-axis has length <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the vertical side has length <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and the enclosed right triangle fills exactly half of the <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />-by-<span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> rectangle those axes mark out. So <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />, the same formula, reached without any new machinery.</p>

