<!-- Section 5: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Legs Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator needs exactly two inputs, entered in order. The steps below follow the on-screen layout from identifying the measurements to a checked result.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the two sides that meet at the 90-degree angle.</strong> These are the legs; the right-angle marker shows where they join.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that neither selected measurement is the hypotenuse.</strong> The hypotenuse is the longest side, opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure the first leg.</strong> Use a tape, ruler, or drawing scale suited to the object.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure the second leg.</strong> Measure along the side itself, corner to corner.</li>
    <li><strong>Make sure both measurements use compatible units.</strong> Metres with metres, feet with feet, never mixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg a in the first input field.</strong> Either leg may go first; the labels are interchangeable.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg b in the second input field.</strong> This is the other perpendicular side.</li>
    <li><strong>Select the appropriate measurement units.</strong> Match the unit to what was actually measured.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong> The result appears with the substitution shown.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the calculated result.</strong> Read the area and confirm that it looks reasonable.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the step-by-step mathematical solution.</strong> It shows the formula, the substitution, and the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the triangle diagram to verify the side labels.</strong> Confirm that a and b sit on the perpendicular sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the result is expressed in square units.</strong> Length units squared, matching the inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Reset when starting a new calculation.</strong> It clears both fields for a clean start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator has exactly two inputs, leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. There are no other modes and no hidden fields; every area on this page comes from <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />.</p>
</section>

