  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />. Step 1: write the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{a\\times b}{2}]]')} />. Step 2: substitute, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}]]')} />. Step 3: multiply, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12}{2}]]')} />. Final answer: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Optional check: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5]]')} />. The hypotenuse confirms the familiar triple but plays no part in the area.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: 6-8-10 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{48}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Rectangle check: the enclosing 6-by-8 rectangle has area 48, and the triangle owns exactly half of it.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times12}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The hypotenuse of 13 never enters the calculation; it would only matter if a side were missing.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: 8-15-17 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=15]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{8\\times15}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=60\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Same three steps, larger numbers, identical logic.</p>

