<!-- Section 8: Pythagorean theorem and area -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is the Pythagorean Theorem Related to Right Triangle Area?</h2>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem states <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}]]]')} />: the legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> determine the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. It answers a question about side lengths, while the area formula measures the enclosed surface. The two are related but solve different problems, and the area calculation here never depends on the theorem.</p>

  <h3>Calculating the Hypotenuse From Both Legs</h3>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{36+64}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=10}]]]')} />. The area is then computed separately: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24]]')} />. The hypotenuse is optional extra information for this area calculation, a useful fact rather than a required step.</p>

  <h3>Can the Pythagorean Theorem Replace the Area Formula?</h3>
  <p>No. The Pythagorean theorem determines relationships between side lengths; the area formula converts two perpendicular sides into enclosed surface. One answers how long, the other answers how much surface. They cooperate: when only one leg and the hypotenuse are known, the theorem recovers the missing leg first, and then the area formula takes over. Neither replaces the other.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Pythagorean Theorem Useful?</h3>
  <p>It earns its place when a side is missing or a measurement needs checking: only one leg and the hypotenuse are known, the missing side must be found before the area step, a calculated hypotenuse needs verification, or a construction measurement must be confirmed on site. For a dedicated missing-side calculation, the Right Triangle Side Calculator handles that step, and its result can feed straight back into the legs area formula.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: Finding a missing leg -->
<section id="sec-missing-leg" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find a Missing Leg When the Triangle Area Is Known</h2>
  <p>The area formula reads both ways. Start with <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />. Multiply both sides by 2: <span set:html={rich('[[2K=ab]]')} />. Divide by <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=\\frac{2K}{a}}]]]')} />. Similarly, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=\\frac{2K}{b}}]]]')} />. The same relationship, rearranged, recovers whichever leg is unknown.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Suppose <span set:html={rich('[[K=24\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\frac{2\\times24}{6}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=8\\text{ cm}}]]]')} />. The units stay consistent: square centimetres divided by centimetres give centimetres. This reverse use is handy for checking a reported area or recovering a dimension from plans, but note it is an educational rearrangement, not an additional input mode in the calculator above, which still takes the two legs.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: How leg lengths affect area -->
<section id="sec-scaling" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Does Changing the Two Legs Affect the Area?</h2>
  <p>Because <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> is a product, stretching the legs stretches the area in predictable ways. The patterns below are worth knowing before resizing any triangular design.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When One Leg Doubles?</h3>
  <p>Keep <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> fixed and double <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K_1=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> becomes <span set:html={rich('[[K_2=\\frac{a(2b)}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_2=2K_1}]]]')} />. Doubling only one leg doubles the area. A concrete check: the 3-by-4 triangle has <span set:html={rich('[[K=6]]')} />; stretching one leg to 3-by-8 gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=12]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Both Legs Double?</h3>
  <p>Now double both: <span set:html={rich('[[K_2=\\frac{(2a)(2b)}{2}]]')} />, which simplifies to <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_2=4K_1}]]]')} />. Doubling both perpendicular dimensions makes the area four times larger, not twice. The 3-by-4 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[K=6]]')} />) scaled to 6-by-8 gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=24]]')} />, exactly four times the original.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Both Legs Are Scaled?</h3>
  <p>Multiply each leg by a scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K_2=\\frac{(ka)(kb)}{2}]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_2=k^2K_1}]]]')} />. Area grows with the square of the scale factor. Practical example: scale the 6-by-8 triangle by 1.5 to get 9-by-12. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{9\\times12}{2}=54]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[24\\times1.5^2=24\\times2.25=54]]')} />. This is why material estimates jump faster than linear dimensions: double every length and four times the sheet is needed.</p>

  <h3>Can Two Different Right Triangles Have the Same Area?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Compare triangle 1, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />. Triangle 2, <span set:html={rich('[[a=2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{2\\times6}{2}=6]]')} />. Different shapes enclose the same area whenever the product of their legs is equal. A short wide triangle and a tall narrow one can cover identical surface, which matters when fitting panels into different spaces.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('same-area-different-triangles.svg')} alt="Two right triangles, 3 by 4 and 2 by 6, shown side by side with equal area 6" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Different proportions, same area: both right triangles enclose 6 square units.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Right triangle vs general triangle -->
<section id="sec-general" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Can&apos;t You Use the Two-Leg Formula for Every Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> works because the two legs are perpendicular. For a general triangle, two side lengths alone are insufficient: the included angle between them, or the corresponding height, must also be known.</p>
  <p>The general rule is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab\\sin(C)}{2}}]]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> is the included angle between sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(90^\\circ)=1]]')} />. Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab\\times1}{2}]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. The right-triangle formula is the general formula with the sine factor equal to 1. For any other angle the sine shrinks the product, which is why the legs formula must never be applied to a non-right triangle: it silently treats <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(C)]]')} /> as 1 when it is not.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Units and decimals -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Units When Calculating Right Triangle Area</h2>
  <p>The two legs must share one length unit before the formula is applied. The square unit in the answer comes from multiplying the two length units, so consistent units are what keep the number meaningful.</p>

  <h3>Why the Result Uses Square Units</h3>
  <p>Area counts unit squares covering the surface: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{cm}\\times\\text{cm}=\\text{cm}^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{m}\\times\\text{m}=\\text{m}^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{ft}\\times\\text{ft}=\\text{ft}^2]]')} />. The unit is always the length unit squared, because the product of two lengths is a count of squares. Reporting an area as 24 m is a category error: 24 m is a length, while 24 m² is a surface.</p>

  <h3>Converting Different Units Before Calculating</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ ft}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=24\\text{ inches}]]')} />. Convert first: <span set:html={rich('[[24\\text{ inches}=2\\text{ ft}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times2}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=3\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. Without conversion, the raw product 72 has no clean meaning: it is neither square feet nor square inches. Convert first, multiply second, and the unit of the answer follows automatically.</p>

  <h3>Calculating With Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>The same formula handles decimals without any change. Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3.5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=9.2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3.5\\times9.2}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{32.2}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=16.1\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Whole numbers, decimals, and fine workshop measurements all flow through the same multiply-and-halve. Keep the decimal places the measurement supports and let the calculator handle the arithmetic.</p>

  <h3>Why Early Rounding Should Be Avoided</h3>
  <p>Round the inputs and the error rides through the multiplication into the answer. A leg measured as 9.2 should not become 9 before multiplying; the lost 0.2 per unit adds up across the product. Keep full precision through the intermediate steps and round only the final result, to the accuracy the measurements justify. Precision kept early is accuracy delivered at the end.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Area From Legs</h2>
  <p>Nearly every wrong answer in this topic traces to a mislabeled input rather than bad arithmetic. The fifteen mistakes below are the ones that appear again and again, each with its cause and its fix.</p>

  <h3>1. Using the Hypotenuse Instead of One of the Legs</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the longest side and never forms the right angle. Entering it as a leg inflates the product, for example treating 10 as a leg with 8 gives 40 instead of 24. Fix: find the right angle first; the two sides touching it are the legs.</p>

  <h3>2. Confusing the Two Legs With the Other Sides</h3>
  <p>In a tilted drawing, any side can look like a natural base. The labels <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> belong to the perpendicular pair only. Fix: locate the square corner marker before assigning labels, regardless of how the triangle is rotated.</p>

  <h3>3. Forgetting That the Legs Must Form a 90-Degree Angle</h3>
  <p>The halved product assumes perpendicularity. Two sides meeting at 80 or 100 degrees need the general sine formula instead. Fix: confirm the corner is square before using <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>4. Forgetting to Divide by Two</h3>
  <p>The product <span set:html={rich('[[a\\times b]]')} /> alone is the enclosing rectangle&apos;s area, double the triangle&apos;s. Fix: halve the product; the /2 is part of the formula, not optional.</p>

  <h3>5. Calculating the Rectangle&apos;s Area Instead of the Triangle&apos;s Area</h3>
  <p>Forty-eight is a valid area for the 6-by-8 rectangle, just not for the triangle inside it. Fix: ask which shape the question wants, then apply the halving only for the triangle.</p>

  <h3>6. Mixing Feet and Inches</h3>
  <p>Multiplying 3 ft by 24 in gives 72 of nothing clean. Fix: convert both to one unit (24 in = 2 ft) before calculating.</p>

  <h3>7. Mixing Centimetres and Metres</h3>
  <p>The same trap at metric scale: 2 m by 30 cm must become one unit first. Fix: convert (30 cm = 0.3 m), then multiply.</p>

  <h3>8. Reporting the Result in Linear Units</h3>
  <p>An area of 24 m² is not 24 m. Fix: write the squared unit with the number, every time.</p>

  <h3>9. Using Only One Leg</h3>
  <p>One leg is a length, not an area; the formula needs the pair. Entering a single measurement answers nothing. Fix: measure both perpendicular sides.</p>

  <h3>10. Treating Any Two Sides of a Right Triangle as Perpendicular</h3>
  <p>Only the legs are perpendicular. A leg-plus-hypotenuse pair meets at an acute angle, so their halved product is not the area. Fix: check that both chosen sides touch the right angle.</p>

  <h3>11. Using Negative or Zero Measurements</h3>
  <p>Lengths are positive. Zero collapses the triangle to a line with no area, and negative lengths have no geometric meaning here. Fix: re-measure; reject non-positive inputs.</p>

  <h3>12. Rounding Decimal Inputs Too Early</h3>
  <p>Rounding 9.2 to 9 before multiplying shifts the answer from 16.1 downward. Fix: keep full precision through the calculation and round only the final result.</p>

  <h3>13. Assuming the Hypotenuse Is Required</h3>
  <p>It is not. <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> uses only the legs, and computing the hypotenuse first adds a step that changes nothing. Fix: skip it unless a different calculation needs it.</p>

  <h3>14. Confusing Perimeter With Area</h3>
  <p>Perimeter adds the three sides (<span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c]]')} />); area multiplies two legs and halves. For 6-8-10, the perimeter is 24 while the area is also 24, a coincidence that causes real confusion. Fix: check whether the question asks for boundary length or enclosed surface.</p>

  <h3>15. Applying the Right-Triangle Formula to a Non-Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Without a 90-degree corner, the product needs the sine of the included angle. Using <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> on a general triangle overstates the area. Fix: use <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab\\sin(C)}{2}]]')} /> with the actual included angle.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24]]')} />. Incorrectly forgetting to divide by two: <span set:html={rich('[[6\\times8=48]]')} />, which is the area of the corresponding rectangle, not the right triangle. Incorrectly using the hypotenuse 10 as if it were a perpendicular leg: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{10\\times8}{2}=40]]')} />, wrong because 10 is not perpendicular to 8. The arithmetic looks confident in all three cases; only the geometry tells them apart.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Area Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Six independent checks, all demonstrated on the 6-8-10 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=24]]')} />). Agreement between different routes is the strongest evidence an answer is right, in homework and in practical measurement alike.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Recalculate Using the Main Formula</h3>
  <p>Run <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> fresh: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24]]')} />. A second pass with the numbers re-entered catches typos and mis-keyed digits.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Double the Triangle to Form a Rectangle</h3>
  <p>The doubled triangle is a 6-by-8 rectangle: <span set:html={rich('[[K_{\\text{rectangle}}=6\\times8=48]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[K_{\\text{triangle}}=\\frac{48}{2}=24]]')} />. If the doubled area does not match <span set:html={rich('[[a\\times b]]')} />, the arithmetic slipped somewhere.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Check the Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{36+64}=10]]')} />. This checks the side identification behind the area; a wrong hypotenuse means a side was mislabeled.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Rearrange the Area Formula</h3>
  <p>Recover a known input from the answer: <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\frac{2K}{a}=\\frac{48}{6}=8]]')} />. Getting back the leg you started with closes the loop and confirms the division step.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Check the Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Both legs must use the same length unit, and the answer must use that unit squared. Mixed units or a linear unit on the answer flag a setup error before any numbers are rechecked.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Estimate the Result</h3>
  <p>Round to friendly numbers: 6 by 8 is near 50, halved near 25. An answer of 24 sits right beside the estimate; an answer of 240 or 2.4 would not. Estimation catches decimal-point and division errors that exact recomputation can repeat.</p>
</section>

